.TH DNS_BUNNY 1 "2026-08-29" "dns_bunny 0.4.1" "SCS Commands"
.SH NAME
dns_bunny.sh \- reconcile project-owned records in an existing Bunny DNS zone
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dns_bunny.sh
.br
.B dns_bunny.sh
.BR help | version
.br
.B dns_bunny.sh init-key
.I keyfile
.br
.B dns_bunny.sh
.BR validate | check | list | plan | apply | verify | prune
.I records.json
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dns_bunny.sh
reads a declarative JSON file, compares its records with an existing Bunny DNS
zone, and manages only records explicitly owned by the declaration's project.
Unrelated zone records and records owned by other projects are preserved.
.PP
With no arguments, the program prints its version and short usage, exits
successfully, and changes nothing.
The ordinary workflow is to run
.BR validate ,
.BR check ,
.BR plan ,
.BR apply ,
and
.BR verify
in that order.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B help
Print usage and exit successfully.
.TP
.B version
Print the program version.
.TP
.BI init-key " keyfile"
Read a Bunny API key from the controlling terminal with echo disabled.
Create the parent directory with mode 0700 and the key file with mode 0600.
An existing file is never overwritten.
.TP
.BI validate " records.json"
Validate the local JSON structure and supported values without reading the key
or contacting Bunny.
.TP
.BI check " records.json"
Validate dependencies, key-file protection, API access, the owner, and exact
zone lookup.
This command is read-only.
.TP
.BI list " records.json"
Print the zone's current public record values and routing fields without record
identifiers, comments, or credentials.
This command is read-only.
.TP
.BI plan " records.json"
Print one OK, CREATE, UPDATE, REPLACE, or CONFLICT action for every declared
record.
This command is read-only.
.TP
.BI apply " records.json"
Refuse every unresolved conflict, back up the current zone document, create or
update declared records, fetch the zone again, and require a clean plan.
The command adopts an exact existing unmanaged record by adding the project's
ownership comment.
An owned record whose name or type changes is replaced because Bunny does not
reliably apply those identity changes through its update endpoint.
.TP
.BI verify " records.json"
Require every declared record to be present with the exact managed value.
This command is read-only.
.TP
.BI prune " records.json"
Require all declared records to verify, back up the zone, and delete only
records bearing this declaration's ownership prefix whose keys are no longer
declared.
This is an explicit destructive command; it never deletes unrelated records.
.SH RECORD FILE
The top-level JSON object contains:
.TP
.B owner
A stable lowercase declaration namespace.
Letters, digits, dots, underscores, and hyphens are accepted, up to 64
characters.
Use a different owner for every independently pruned declaration in the same
zone.
.TP
.B zone
The existing Bunny DNS zone name, such as
.BR example.com .
.TP
.B key_file
An absolute path to the one-line Bunny API key file.
.TP
.B backup_dir
An absolute local directory for protected pre-change zone documents.
.TP
.B records
A non-empty array of desired record objects.
.PP
Every record requires a unique lowercase
.BR key ,
a supported
.BR type ,
a zone-relative
.BR name ,
and a non-empty
.BR value .
The name is empty or
.B @
for the zone apex.
.PP
Optional fields are
.BR ttl ,
.BR priority ,
.BR weight ,
.BR port ,
.BR flags ,
.BR tag ,
.BR disabled ,
and
.BR allow_multiple .
TTL defaults to 300 seconds when omitted.
Numeric routing fields default to zero.
.PP
Supported standard types are A, AAAA, CNAME, TXT, MX, SRV, CAA, PTR, NS,
SVCB, HTTPS, and TLSA.
Bunny's Redirect, Flatten, PullZone, and Script records require additional
product-specific fields and are outside this declaration format.
.SH OWNERSHIP
Managed records receive a Bunny comment in this form:
.PP
.RS
managed-by:OWNER; key=KEY
.RE
.PP
The owner isolates projects sharing one zone.
The unique key is the durable identity used across value or TTL changes.
Do not reuse an owner in two independently pruned declarations; either
declaration could delete owned records omitted from itself.
Changing an owner does not rename its records; the old records become
unmanaged from the new declaration's perspective.
If more than one record has the same ownership comment, the program reports a
conflict and changes nothing.
.PP
An exact unmanaged record may be adopted because adding the comment does not
change its DNS answer.
A differing unmanaged record set causes a conflict unless the declaration
explicitly permits multiple records.
CNAME exclusivity is always enforced.
.PP
.B apply
and
.B prune
assume that no other process, API client, or dashboard edits the same zone
while the command runs.
Post-apply verification detects a divergent declared state but cannot lock the
zone or prevent a concurrent request.
.SH API KEY
The key is a Bunny account API credential.
Keep it on the trusted operator workstation and out of version control.
Never provide it as a command-line argument or store it in the record file.
.PP
The key file must be a regular, non-symbolic file with mode 0400 or 0600 and
exactly one line of printable ASCII without leading or trailing spaces.
The program places the AccessKey request header in a temporary mode-0600 file
so the key does not appear in curl's process arguments.
Temporary request files are removed on normal exit and handled HUP, INT, or
TERM signals.
SIGKILL cannot run the cleanup trap; a protected mode-0700 temporary directory
and mode-0600 request files may remain under TMPDIR after such a termination.
.SH BACKUPS
Before
.B apply
changes any record, or
.B prune
deletes any record, the current Bunny zone document is copied to
.I backup_dir
with mode 0600.
The directory is mode 0700.
These files include public DNS data and Bunny record identifiers, but not the
API key.
An existing same-second backup is never overwritten.
.SH PORTABILITY
The program uses POSIX shell syntax and supports key-file mode checks on macOS,
Linux, and SmartOS.
It requires curl, jq 1.6 or newer, stat, mktemp, and ordinary POSIX utilities.
The validator checks for the jq IN filter before reading a declaration.
.PP
The supplied GNU Makefile installs to
.I $HOME/bin
and
.I $HOME/share/man/man1
on macOS.
On SmartOS it requires the global zone and installs to
.I /opt/custom/bin
and
.I /opt/custom/share/man/man1
by default.
Use
.B gmake
on SmartOS.
Absolute BINDIR and MANDIR overrides and an absolute DESTDIR staging prefix are
supported.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
Usage, validation, dependency, permission, network, API, conflict, or
verification failure.
.TP
.B 130
The command received HUP, INT, or TERM and ran its signal cleanup handler.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I records.example.json
Copyable declaration schema with non-secret example values.
.TP
.I README.org
Quickstart, safety model, installation instructions, and integration guidance.
.SH SECURITY
The tool never creates or deletes a Bunny zone.
It does not alter registrar delegation, DNSSEC, or unrelated records.
HTTP tracing and key-bearing debug output are not provided.
.PP
Review
.B plan
before every
.B apply
and retain an off-project copy of important DNS exports.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR curl (1),
.BR jq (1)
